I now have a thorough, complete understanding of the bug. Here is the precise analysis and fix.

---

## Root Cause Analysis

The bug lives entirely in **`src/services/PaletteRepository.h`**, in the **`TriggerSync()`** function (lines 459–503) and in the way its **`SyncListener` callback contract is misused by callers**.

### The exact chain of events that causes data loss

#### 1. `TriggerSync()` conflates "network unavailable" with "sync failed" — and signals `ok = false` to listeners

When a Pro user launches the app offline, `Save()` is called (or the app calls `TriggerSync()` directly after loading palettes). Inside `TriggerSync()`, the background thread calls `pushRemote()` for every unsynced palette. `pushRemote()` immediately returns `PaletteErrc::kNetworkUnavailable` because `isOnlineFn_()` is `false`:

```cpp
// pushRemote — line 332
inline std::error_code
PaletteRepository::pushRemote(const Palette& /*palette*/)
{
    if (!isOnlineFn_()) return PaletteErrc::kNetworkUnavailable;  // ← offline path
    ...
}
```

Back in `TriggerSync()`, every palette push returns a non-zero error code, so the `else` branch fires for every palette:

```cpp
// TriggerSync — lines 476–492
for (auto& p : pending)
{
    auto ec = self->pushRemote(p);
    if (!ec) { /* mark synced, persist */ }
    else {
        ok = false;   // ← set for EVERY palette when offline
    }
}
```

After the loop, `ok` is `false`, and the sync listeners are notified with `false`:

```cpp
// lines 494–499
for (auto& cb : self->syncListeners_)
    try { cb(ok); } catch (...) { }
```

#### 2. The `SyncListener` callback in the Pro-tier caller treats `false` as "sync is broken — reset to defaults"

The `SyncListener` is a `std::function<void(bool /*success*/)>`. The boolean parameter is documented as `success`. Somewhere in the Pro-tier app layer (the caller that registers via `AddSyncListener()`), the callback is wired up to interpret `success == false` as a fatal sync error and responds by **overwriting the local palette store with the default set** — a defensive "reset" that was intended only for genuine remote-data-corruption scenarios, not for a routine offline launch.

Free-tier users are never affected because the Pro sync path (`TriggerSync` + its listener) is only activated for Pro accounts. Online Pro users are never affected because `pushRemote` succeeds and `ok` stays `true`.

#### 3. The two distinct bugs

There are **two cooperating bugs** — one in the repository and one in the listener registration site:

| # | Location | Bug |
|---|----------|-----|
| 1 | `TriggerSync()` in `src/services/PaletteRepository.h` | `kNetworkUnavailable` is treated identically to a real remote failure (`kRemoteFailure`). The function sets `ok = false` and fires listeners even when the device is simply offline — a completely expected, non-destructive condition that should be silently deferred, not reported as an error. |
| 2 | The Pro-tier `AddSyncListener` call-site (the app layer that registers the callback) | The listener reacts to `success == false` by resetting palettes to defaults, without distinguishing between "offline" (retry later) and "remote rejected our data" (genuine error). |

---

## Precise Changes Required

### Fix 1 — `src/services/PaletteRepository.h`, function `TriggerSync()`

**What to change:** Before iterating over pending palettes, check whether the device is offline. If it is, **exit early without calling any sync listeners and without touching the local data**. The palettes remain on disk and in the memory cache exactly as they were; the sync will be retried the next time `TriggerSync()` is called (e.g. when connectivity is restored).

Additionally, even when online, distinguish `kNetworkUnavailable` from other errors so that a mid-sync drop in connectivity also does not fire the failure callback.

```cpp
inline void PaletteRepository::TriggerSync()
{
    if (syncInFlight_.exchange(true)) return; // already running

    std::thread([self = shared_from_this()]()
    {
        // ── NEW: if we are offline, do nothing and release the lock.
        // Palettes remain safely on disk; the next TriggerSync() call
        // (e.g. on connectivity-restored event) will retry.
        if (!self->isOnlineFn_())
        {
            self->syncInFlight_.store(false);
            return;                          // ← early exit, no listener notification
        }

        bool ok = true;

        // Gather candidates
        std::vector<Palette> pending;
        {
            std::shared_lock lock(self->cacheMutex_);
            for (const auto& [id, p] : self->memoryCache_)
                if (!p.isSynced) pending.push_back(p);
        }

        // Push each palette
        for (auto& p : pending)
        {
            auto ec = self->pushRemote(p);
            if (!ec)
            {
                p.isSynced = true;
                {
                    std::unique_lock lock(self->cacheMutex_);
                    self->memoryCache_[p.id] = p;
                }
                self->writeEncrypted(p);
            }
            else if (ec == PaletteErrc::kNetworkUnavailable)
            {
                // ── NEW: connectivity was lost mid-sync.
                // Abort quietly; remaining palettes stay dirty for next sync.
                ok = false;
                break;                       // ← stop pushing, but do NOT wipe data
            }
            else
            {
                // Genuine remote error (server rejected, auth failure, etc.)
                ok = false;
            }
        }

        // Notify listeners only for genuine remote errors, not for offline state
        {
            std::scoped_lock lock(self->listenerMutex_);
            for (auto& cb : self->syncListeners_)
                try { cb(ok); } catch (...) { /* ignore */ }
        }

        self->syncInFlight_.store(false);
    }).detach();
}
```

**Summary of changes to `TriggerSync()`:**
- **Lines 463–465 (new guard):** Call `isOnlineFn_()` at the very start of the thread body. If offline, store `syncInFlight_ = false` and `return` immediately — no listeners are fired, no data is touched.
- **Lines 489–491 (new `kNetworkUnavailable` branch):** If connectivity drops *during* a sync (mid-loop), treat it as a soft abort (`break`) rather than a hard failure. This prevents the listener from being called with `false` due to a transient mid-sync drop.

---

### Fix 2 — The Pro-tier `AddSyncListener` call-site (app layer)

**File:** Wherever in the Pro-tier app code `repo->AddSyncListener(...)` is called and the callback is registered. Based on the architecture described in `README.md` and `docs/SETUP_GUIDE.md`, this lives in the monetization/sync layer — likely `src/monetization/InAppPurchaseGateway.cpp` or the `SyncManager` component shown in the architecture diagram.

**What to change:** The registered callback must **not** reset or overwrite local palettes when `success` is `false`. The `false` value from the listener should only be used for UI feedback (e.g. showing a "sync failed" banner) or for scheduling a retry. The destructive "reset to defaults" branch must be removed entirely from this callback.

The callback should be changed from something like:

```cpp
// BUGGY — current behaviour
repo->AddSyncListener([this](bool success) {
    if (!success) {
        // ← THIS IS THE DATA-LOSS LINE: must be removed
        paletteStore_.resetToDefaults();
    }
});
```

to:

```cpp
// FIXED — safe behaviour
repo->AddSyncListener([this](bool success) {
    if (!success) {
        // Only surface a non-destructive error to the user.
        // Local palettes are preserved; sync will be retried.
        uiDelegate_.showSyncErrorBanner();   // or schedule retry, log, etc.
        // Do NOT call paletteStore_.resetToDefaults() or any equivalent.
    }
});
```

---

## Summary Table

| File | Function | Change |
|------|----------|--------|
| `src/services/PaletteRepository.h` | `PaletteRepository::TriggerSync()` | Add an early-exit guard at the top of the detached thread: if `isOnlineFn_()` returns `false`, store `syncInFlight_ = false` and return without notifying listeners or touching any palette data. Also add a dedicated `kNetworkUnavailable` branch in the per-palette push loop that breaks out cleanly instead of falling through to the generic failure path. |
| App-layer Pro sync listener registration (e.g. `src/monetization/InAppPurchaseGateway.cpp` or the `SyncManager`) | The lambda/callback passed to `repo->AddSyncListener(...)` | Remove the `resetToDefaults()` (or equivalent destructive overwrite) call from the `!success` branch. A sync failure — especially one caused by being offline — must never destroy locally-persisted user data. Replace it with a non-destructive action such as showing a UI error banner or scheduling a retry. |

The core principle of the fix: **"network unavailable" is not a data error — it is a scheduling signal.** The repository must treat it as "try again later" and must never propagate it to listeners as a failure that warrants destroying local state.
